     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still haven't entered it. A confirmation alone doesn't change the safety problem, and I'm not comfortable submitting this dose without more information.\n\n5,100 mg a day is twice the maximum labeled dose of metformin. I don't know of any accepted use for a dose that high. Above the maximum, the risk of lactic acidosis goes up without any added effect on blood sugar. In most hospitals, an order like this would also be stopped at pharmacist review.\n\n**Ways to move forward:**\n- **If there's a specific clinical reason** for this dose, such as a research protocol or a specialist's recommendation, tell me what it is. I can enter the order with that reason in the note and flag it for pharmacist review, so the decision is documented and checked by a second person.\n- **If the goal is maximum metformin,** I can enter 850 mg by mouth three times daily with meals (2,550 mg a day) right now.\n- **If blood sugar still isn't controlled at the maximum dose,** the usual next step is adding another drug, such as basal insulin, which is common for inpatients.\n\nLet me know which you'd like and I'll enter it right away."
      }
     ],
